    <!-- Aggregating across subjects -->
    <h3 style="font-size: 20px; font-weight: 800; color: var(--app-text); margin-bottom: 16px;">📝 How to Calculate Aggregate Percentage Across Subjects</h3>
    <div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 14px; margin-bottom: 36px;">
      <div style="background: var(--app-surface-card); border: 1px solid var(--app-border); border-radius: 14px; padding: 20px;">
        <h4 style="font-size: 15px; font-weight: 700; color: var(--app-text); margin-bottom: 10px;">Example: CBSE Class 12 (5 subjects × 100 marks each = 500 total)</h4>
        <p style="font-size: 14px; color: var(--app-text-secondary); line-height: 1.8; margin: 0;">
          English: 87 | Physics: 92 | Chemistry: 78 | Maths: 95 | Computer: 89<br />
          <strong>Total scored = 87 + 92 + 78 + 95 + 89 = 441</strong><br />
          <strong>Percentage = (441 ÷ 500) × 100 = 88.2%</strong>
        </p>
      </div>
      <div style="background: var(--app-surface-card); border: 1px solid var(--app-border); border-radius: 14px; padding: 20px;">
        <h4 style="font-size: 15px; font-weight: 700; color: var(--app-text); margin-bottom: 10px;">Example: Kerala / TN Plus Two (6 subjects, max 1200)</h4>
        <p style="font-size: 14px; color: var(--app-text-secondary); line-height: 1.8; margin: 0;">
          If you scored 1050 out of 1200:<br />
          <strong>Percentage = (1050 ÷ 1200) × 100 = 87.5%</strong><br />
          This is in A grade (Distinction) as per Tamil Nadu / Kerala grading.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- FAQs — GSC query matched -->
    <h3 style="font-size: 20px; font-weight: 800; color: var(--app-text); margin-bottom: 20px;">❓ Frequently Asked Questions</h3>
    <div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 14px; margin-bottom: 32px;">

      <div style="background: var(--app-surface-card); padding: 20px 24px; border-radius: 14px; border: 1px solid var(--app-border);">
        <h4 style="font-size: 15px; font-weight: 700; color: var(--app-text); margin-bottom: 8px;">How to calculate Plus Two (Class 12) percentage?</h4>
        <p style="font-size: 14px; color: var(--app-text-secondary); margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">Add marks from all subjects, divide by maximum marks, multiply by 100. For Kerala/TN Plus Two (1200 max): Percentage = (Total Scored ÷ 1200) × 100. For CBSE (500 max): Percentage = (Total ÷ 500) × 100.</p>
      </div>

      <div style="background: var(--app-surface-card); padding: 20px 24px; border-radius: 14px; border: 1px solid var(--app-border);">
        <h4 style="font-size: 15px; font-weight: 700; color: var(--app-text); margin-bottom: 8px;">Percentage calculator for Plus Two — which subjects are counted?</h4>
        <p style="font-size: 14px; color: var(--app-text-secondary); margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">For CBSE: Best 5 of 6 subjects count (if applicable). For TN/Kerala Plus Two: All 6 subjects × 200 marks each (theory + practical) = 1200 total. For Karnataka PUC: All 6 subjects × 100 = 600 marks total.</p>
      </div>

      <div style="background: var(--app-surface-card); padding: 20px 24px; border-radius: 14px; border: 1px solid var(--app-border);">
        <h4 style="font-size: 15px; font-weight: 700; color: var(--app-text); margin-bottom: 8px;">How to calculate Madhyamik (WB Class 10) percentage?</h4>
        <p style="font-size: 14px; color: var(--app-text-secondary); margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">West Bengal Madhyamik (WBBSE) is marked out of 700 (7 subjects × 100 each). Percentage = (Total Marks ÷ 700) × 100. Scoring 560/700 = 80% (First Class with distinction). The pass mark is 33% per subject.</p>
      </div>

      <div style="background: var(--app-surface-card); padding: 20px 24px; border-radius: 14px; border: 1px solid var(--app-border);">
        <h4 style="font-size: 15px; font-weight: 700; color: var(--app-text); margin-bottom: 8px;">How to calculate HSC percentage (Maharashtra Board)?</h4>
        <p style="font-size: 14px; color: var(--app-text-secondary); margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">Maharashtra HSC (Class 12) is out of 600 marks (6 subjects × 100). Percentage = (Total ÷ 600) × 100. Marks include theory + practical components per subject. 60%+ = First Class. 75%+ = Distinction.</p>
      </div>

      <div style="background: var(--app-surface-card); padding: 20px 24px; border-radius: 14px; border: 1px solid var(--app-border);">
        <h4 style="font-size: 15px; font-weight: 700; color: var(--app-text); margin-bottom: 8px;">How to calculate 10th result percentage?</h4>
        <p style="font-size: 14px; color: var(--app-text-secondary); margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">CBSE Class 10 is out of 500 (5 subjects). State Boards vary: UP Board (500), Maharashtra SSC (600), WB Madhyamik (700), TN SSLC (600). Formula is always: (Total Scored ÷ Max Marks) × 100.</p>
      </div>

      <div style="background: var(--app-surface-card); padding: 20px 24px; border-radius: 14px; border: 1px solid var(--app-border);">
        <h4 style="font-size: 15px; font-weight: 700; color: var(--app-text); margin-bottom: 8px;">What is a good percentage in board exams?</h4>
        <p style="font-size: 14px; color: var(--app-text-secondary); margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;"><strong>90%+</strong>: Excellent — qualifies for top college merit lists, scholarships, JEE/NEET preparation cutoffs. <strong>75%–90%</strong>: Good — First Class, most undergraduate admissions. <strong>60%–75%</strong>: Average — Second class, still eligible for most colleges. <strong>Below 60%</strong>: May restrict options for competitive streams like Engineering, Medicine, and Commerce Honours.</p>
      </div>

      <div style="background: var(--app-surface-card); padding: 20px 24px; border-radius: 14px; border: 1px solid var(--app-border);">
        <h4 style="font-size: 15px; font-weight: 700; color: var(--app-text); margin-bottom: 8px;">How to check percentage of marks in college exams?</h4>
        <p style="font-size: 14px; color: var(--app-text-secondary); margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">For university semester exams: Add marks from all subjects/papers. Divide by the maximum marks of all papers combined. Multiply by 100. For CGPA-based systems, the university provides an official formula (e.g., CGPA × 7.1 for Mumbai University).</p>
      </div>

    </div>

  </article>
</Layout>


